  "panel_type": "Four new fixed ASCII prose probes composed for this study. Not a random corpus sample or training-held-out data. Selected before checkpoint inference.",
  "panels": [
    {
      "name": "water_cycle",
      "text": "The water in a small lake changes throughout the year as rain, evaporation, and streams alter its depth. A field team visits the same shore every week to measure temperature and record the weather. They place a ruler against a fixed stone rather than relying on the apparent position of the water from a photograph. In spring, snow melting in the hills adds water even on days without rain. During a warm summer, evaporation may remove more water than the streams supply. The team compares measurements from different seasons and keeps a separate note of unusual storms. These observations help them distinguish a brief change in the weather from a sustained change in the local water cycle. They also inspect the reeds, take photographs, and record whether the ground is dry.",
