    {
      "name": "library",
      "text": "The public library keeps several old maps in a cabinet near the reading room. Before a map is moved, a member of staff checks its catalogue number and records the condition of the paper. Some sheets have small tears along their edges, while others have faded labels or repairs made many years ago. A conservator works on one map at a time and uses a clean support to prevent new folds. Readers can consult digital copies while the original sheets are being treated. The library plans to show a small selection in an exhibition about the town and its surrounding farms. The exhibition notes explain how roads and waterways changed, and they distinguish details recorded on the maps from later interpretations of the landscape.",
