    {
      "name": "bread",
      "text": "The first batch of bread was ready shortly after sunrise. The baker had mixed flour and water the previous evening, leaving enough time for the dough to develop before adding the remaining ingredients. A cooler room slows the process, so the schedule is adjusted according to the season rather than followed exactly every day. After the dough has risen, it is divided into pieces of equal weight and shaped on a lightly floured surface. The loaves rest again before going into the oven. Steam helps keep their surfaces flexible during the first part of baking. When the crust has browned, the baker checks that the bread is cooked and lets it cool on a rack. Notes about temperature, timing, and texture make it easier to compare the next batch.",
